INSERT INTO "entries" ("id", "project_id", "user_id", "date", "title", "summary", "description", "created_at", "updated_at") VALUES (1,2,1,E'2026-09-13',E'LOG KEEPER - V_1.0.0',E'Here is the first version of the website. There\'s a lot of things that\'s going to be added inside of the website.',E'Hi, here\'s Chab.\r\n\r\nAs said in the summary, there\'s going to be a lot of things that\'s going to change on the website. Such as the UI, it was made with AI since I wanted to make it quickly. Also, its main use will be for the school project it was made for. \r\n\r\nUpdates to come : \r\n- UI overhaul\r\n- Possibility to delete a whole project\r\n- Username will appear on projects and logs\r\n- The removal of the field name, it\'s redundant\r\n- Line return for the summary and description\r\n- The implementation of a CI/CD pipeline\r\n- Be able to have a description that looks like a markdown\r\n- Other things I will think in the mean time',E'2026-09-13 22:46:30',E'2026-09-14 11:39:03'),(2,3,1,E'2026-09-15',E'Design Notebook - 1',E'The design notebook is being concieved.',E'The biggest part of the design notebook is done. An essential version of the document was made with Claude compacting the most important information into ten pages or less. The full version is made up to 70%. The due date for the final document is next week for the class of week five, at midnight.',E'2026-09-15 16:26:31',E'2026-09-16 21:06:39'),(3,3,1,E'2026-09-16',E'Design Notebook - 2',E'The design notebook has progressed during today. It is still not done.',E'The document has progressed. I am currently at the designing of the diagram for the physical connections beetween the STM32, ESP32, the driver and motors. It takes some times as everything does, but with diligent work, the design notebook will be done for the due date. To accelerate the work, I made a prompt for the built-in AI that Miro has, but it\'s quite bad at his work. Thus it will need to be made by hand. The next steps after that will be to give explanation on how the different signal a going in/out of the STM32, such as the PWM signal, the CS output from the drivers, the directions outputs and the low filter logical pass.',E'2026-09-16 21:06:25',E'2026-09-16 21:06:25'),(4,3,1,E'2026-09-22',E'Design Notebook - 3',E'Finalization of the design notebook and conversion of the .docx original file into .pdf file format.',E'Today, i\'ve finished the redaction of the design notebook. I have also converted it into a pdf as requested from my teacher. Unrealated to the notebook, I have tried out on the network of the cegep the MQTT network design I wanted for the projects. Luckily for me, it works out without any issues and is able to communicate with the computer without any real problem. Although, I need to set the IP of the device running the server into the ESP32 .ino script. But that\'s an issue that can be easily resolve with a initialization script that takes the IP from the PC serving the server and putting it into the .ino file. Then after the ESP32 is flashed without any issue. And also it resolve the problem of having the two devices know eachother IP\'s. Furthermore, the initalization script could be made so it also gives the Wifi credentials that the device running the server uses so there is no worries about which network is the ESP32 on. Effectively, now I can really start working on the project.',E'2026-09-22 17:22:28',E'2026-09-22 17:23:35'),(5,3,1,E'2026-09-24',E'Design Notebook - 4',E'This log was suppossed to be made the sept. 23 2026. Made the power point for the presentation and final verification of the design notebook.',E'Made the power point by Claude and manually revised it for the project presentation on the 30 september. The pptx is only 8 pages long with the first and last page being greeting and thank you pages. So the 6 other pages are use fully used while keeping the disposition clean.',E'2026-09-24 19:50:53',E'2026-09-24 19:57:16'),(6,3,1,E'2026-09-25',E'3D Design - 1',E'The start of the process of the 3D design of the pieces. Such as the cradle and rudder.',E'Today, I have made the rudder 3D design and printed the piece along the way to test the dimension. Some adjustment for the holes had to be done, but the other dimensions were correct. So the rudder is done. I\'ve also started working on the cradle for the motors, redesigning them to supports screws. It\'s in the goal of being able of easily assembling the whole mechanism. The cradle at the time of writing this log is not done just yet. There\'s is still some details I need to work on like the fixation of it to the base for the hand. There\'s also the cap for the cradles and the screw holes that will be aligned directly with the motors. But, they can be screwed in place together now. For the material needed to screw them, I already have the necessary brass insert and screws for the prototype. An other detail I need to look over is passing the wires of the motors in an efficient way trough the cradle without being an hurdle to the whole mechanism.',E'2026-09-25 16:11:45',E'2026-09-25 16:11:45'),(7,6,1,E'2026-10-06',E'Status - Checkpoint #1',E'I have done most of the work I needed. The lobbies work,the main page is completed, the auth via OAuth an normal login is working. The actual races work in prod with up to 60 bots, I can\'t try with humans. The only thing I really need to do is the bonus.',E'I have upward to 200~ commits. That\'s due to the Agent workflow I\'ve made. It\'s allowed to push on dev and make branches from it. Also merge in dev. On the repo, most of the code is the claude skills for Vercel. Probably the next step is going to remove the unused and bloated software that\'s just sitting in the repo. At the time of writing this, it\'s roughly 50% of the project alone. And I\'ve moved away from Vercel as my deployment platform. Thus making those skill useless. Apart from that, I need to do the checkpoint #1, so that\'s making the repo public and adding the design .md and other needed file for it. There is some fixes I need to do, like the leader board not showing the users. Here is the link : https://jjkcursetyper-production.up.railway.app/',E'2026-10-06 13:39:47',E'2026-10-06 13:42:17'),(8,3,1,E'2026-10-13',E'3D Design - Design 2',E'The design for most of the pieces is done, there\'s only just the base that needs some work',E'Every pieces except for the base is done. Ready to be printed. There\'s just the base that need some work on it, but that shouldn\'t be much of an issue. It\'s going to take a few days before every pieces are printed. For each cradle, I am looking forward to at least 10h of printing if not more. The rudders are going to be the most fastest to be printed. Then there\'s the tendon, I need ten of them, each pair for a motor. And with them being different sizes, that going to take some time. So I am starting to print at least one full finger, the cradle, tendons and rudder to make sure it\'s good.',E'2026-10-13 17:10:20',E'2026-10-13 22:05:34'),(9,3,1,E'2026-10-13',E'3D Printing - Test #1',E'I\'ve tried out a new buse nozzle.',E'During the summer I bought new nozzle for my 3D printer. From 1mm to 0.2mm and what\'s in between. Today, I\'ve changed the nozzle to be an 0.8mm, due to the printing time for the rudder being 6h with a 0.2mm nozzle. Making it 0.8mm brought it down to 35min. A lot less, but the quality isn\'t the greatest strength of this diameter. The pieces I got were horrendous quality wise. Also, the infill and diameter for the tendons were way off their dimension. Just an example for that is my inability of inserting the tendons or motor shaft into the rudders. At least, I got a pretty good idea of what a 0.8mm can do and cannot. Furthermore, I\'ve understand its use to be for big pieces with minimal details and precise dimensions. I don\'t think I\'ll be able to use this nozzle for any pieces apart of the base and maybe the cradles. For my next test, I\'ll redo the rudders but with the 0.4mm nozzle I have.',E'2026-10-13 21:14:17',E'2026-10-13 22:05:06'),(10,6,1,E'2026-10-13',E'Post Checkpoint #1',E'Post checkpoint log',E'I don\'t have the grade for the first checkpoint. Although, I am pretty sure I am going to have quite a good grade on the checkpoint. There\'s only a few things missing from the final product, like the archive page that needs its design and the whole gamification of the website and runs.',E'2026-10-13 22:08:42',E'2026-10-13 22:08:42'),(11,3,1,E'2026-10-14',E'3D Design - Test 2',E'Results of the second print of the rudders',E'I\'ve tried printing the rudder with the 0.4mm nozzle instead of the 0.8mm one. The result is much more satisfying than what I expected. Out of the five rudders I\'ve printed, only one has a critical flaw. One of the ends is slopped like if the PLA just melted or that the nozzle pushed on the piece a bit. The other rudders do still have filament bridges between them and inside of the motor shaft hole, but it\'s not really an issue. The motor shaft still fits inside of the rudder, even the melted one. And also the tendons do still fit the pieces correctly. From the test I did with the different nozzle, I think I\'ll be able to use the 0.8mm nozzle to make the base, cradles and covers. The 0.4mm for the rudders and the 0.2mm nozzle for the tendons. For the tendons, I need to use the 0.2mm because of the little pusher for the metal knob of the hands tendons. Apart from that, I might also need a piece to keep the MCUs in place and also the drivers.',E'2026-10-14 00:17:52',E'2026-10-14 00:17:52'),(12,3,1,E'2026-10-14',E'3D Design - Plan',E'Plan for the pieces to hold the MCUs and drivers.',E'For the drivers, it\'s going to be quite simple, there\'s already screw holes made to hold down the component in place, so a  small extension from the base to have a seat for the component and it\'s good. Pretty much the same for the MCUs, but a more thought will be needed for their placements and how the cable are going to be placed out.',E'2026-10-14 00:22:29',E'2026-10-14 00:22:29'),(13,3,1,E'2026-10-14',E'3D Design - Cover',E'Printing the covers',E'Today, I\'ll be printing the covers for the different cradles and their cover. I want to have at least a pair done by the end of the day. So that I can get a real feel of how the pieces hold up together when assembled. The first cover that I am making is at 40% completion. The print should take about 45-50min top to finish. The after, for the cradle, it will be complete in a bit less than two hours. Although there is some bridging, it\'s not all that bad since the piece is quite large. Also, I am using the  0.8mm nozzle and seems I was right. It the perfect one for the cover.',E'2026-10-14 15:37:46',E'2026-10-14 15:37:46'),(14,3,1,E'2026-10-14',E'Print result - Cover',E'The result for the print of the cover for the cradles.',E'The cover came out better than I expected. The arch is relatively not bent downward. Obviously, due to having it made with the 0.8mm nozzle. It had some stringing and unwanted filament bridges, but nothing that the cleanup afterward couldn\'t discard with ease. Also, the supports were quite dense, I might need to tweak the density parameters for them. It made it a pain to remove them. Especially the ones at the base of the arch. I sanded down the base to remove the filament from the supports. It went well. So now I a m ready to print the first cradle.',E'2026-10-14 16:41:23',E'2026-10-14 16:41:23'),(15,8,2,E'2026-10-14',E'Start of the assignment',E'started dev work on the assignment',E'I started working on the assignment and it\'s going bad. The coins I\'m supposed to make are not actually working and they won\'t move up and down, they will only move up. I just started programming so the character is non existent at this point in time and when I\'m done with the coins, the assignment will be complete.',E'2026-10-14 18:31:36',E'2026-10-14 18:31:36'),(16,3,1,E'2026-10-21',E'3D Printing - Progress',E'Progress on printing the pieces for the hand.',E'I\'ve started to print out the necessary pieces for the hand base the cradles. I still need to model and print 3 three important pieces, the first is the base that holds the different motors. I have split the whole base in three, the middle part holds the hand, the two others on the sides hold the motor. I need to make a model to hold the driver, and a other model to hold the two MCUs. They\'ll be aligned in a way so the most amount of cable coming into the STM MCU won\'t cause that big of an issue when assembling the whole thing. I am also testing ridges into the bases so the cable aren\'t messing with one another and the final product of the cable looks like traces on a circuit board.',E'2026-10-21 01:28:07',E'2026-10-21 01:28:31');
